# The broad catch-all syslog. Not one named file: a daemon that logs through
# syslog rarely gets a file of its own, it gets whichever of the broad ones its
# facility is routed to... messages, daemon, or syslog, and postgres arrives on
# LOCAL0 while openvpn arrives on DAEMON, so on one box these may be split across
# two or three files and on another all be in one. Point a watcher at each broad
# log you have and give it this group; a rule whose daemon is not in that file
# costs a string compare and nothing more.
#
# Keyed to the log rather than to a kind of software, the way %syslog/mail% is
# keyed to a maillog... which is why there is no group of "the panel rules" or
# "the database rules". Nobody has a log shaped like that.
#
# Large on purpose, and cheap for it. A rule's daemon gate is checked before any
# of its regexps, so each line of a busy shared log pays a handful of string
# compares and only the one rule whose daemon matches ever runs a pattern.
#
# LEFT OUT ON PURPOSE, and not oversights:
#
#   syslog/pam-generic, syslog/drupal-auth
#     Both gate on //.//, any daemon at all, so they get nothing from the daemon
#     gate and run their patterns over every line here. pam-generic would also
#     double-count against sshd and the mail rules on a shared log, where the
#     same PAM failure is already somebody's business. Add them by name if you
#     want them, knowing that.
#
#   syslog/named-refused
#     Not for where it lands but for what it says: its own header warns that a
#     refusal can be perfectly innocent depending on how named is configured, so
#     it wants a decision rather than a group that drops in whole. Add it by name
#     once you know your own config.
#
#   the readers, the sshd mode variants, and the distinct-cardinality rules
#     Opt-in by listing, each for its own reason. See rules.md.
